es and also the same  result extends to the case where you  don't don't just do denoising what  you're doing a regression so you have  the measurement map in here and you have  noise and you're using the regularizer  to reconstruct so this is a typical  estimation problem in statistics again  with multiple structures combining  regular Reiser's hits the same issue ok  so this brings us to I think what's the  most interesting part in the talk which  is this problem is still open what does  one do when you have multiple structures  in one object so we showed a very  general negative result a whole bunch of  things don't work right but what can  work so likely there it would be a  case-by-case solution for each type of  structure but one of the the most  popular combination of structures is low  rank and sparse so matrix that is  jointly low rank and sparse and by the  way I want to point out that this  relaxing  the Lawrences and sparseness  individually using your 1-norm and  nuclear Nord also appears in many  typical SCP relaxations where implicitly  being lower rank is relaxed to being in  the PSD cone as a constraint and then  there is some like trace that is being  minimized that's basically equivalent to  no clear norm for symmetric and PSD  matrices so whenever you have x+ 7f and  under trace in the objective and at the  same time you want some kind of an entry  way structure and you use our one norm  or summation or equality constraints on  the entries usually this means you are  handling your entry wise part of this  structure and the spectral part of the  structure separately and perhaps this is  why a lot of these STP relaxations have  limitations so very very very but so  what can one do in each case one can  first try to find a better penalty maybe  the combination of the individual norms  isn't the best thing so one thing is  going back to that atomic norm framework  and see if one can identify the right  atoms sector convex hull and see if one  can get anything that's definitely  possible if it can be done however for  example for sports on dornan case it's  actually interactable to do then people  have proposed different sort of  heuristic we build approximations to try  to improve and then there have been some  improvements but not otherwise you still  stuck in the same order what constants  have been improved for specific problems  of combining law Rankin spars and then  this one was for unfolding of tensors  the low to high rank tensor and also  another line of work has looked at their  measurements if you if you have the  ability or freedom to construct the  measurement map in a very specific way  you can get around this problem but  usually that's not available so for  example if you can do nested  measurements you first make measurements  that respect the sparsity and then on  top of that make measurements that  respect the low rank and so on so that's  possible but only very restricted  applications would allow such a thing  now one interesting thing is that that I  have been thinking about since the  recent workshop in the in this program  about as well as hierarchies and they're  very recent paper that actually  a lot of people here have worked on yes  Hopkins store so either and others  actually shows on the problem of sparse  PCA which is an example of a problem  that has low ranked transport structures  together that there are exponential  lower bounds on SOS algorithms and so it  kind of hints this is a very hard  problem what would be interesting to see  if this machinery can be also used for  other combinations of structures either  give positive or negative results  whether gaps can be bridged or they  cannot be with point normal time  algorithms so I think that's very  interesting  open question and with that I would like  to just thank everyone first the  collaborators and also Simon's Institute  for this amazing program and thank you  all for listening  [Applause]
 

